From be94641305ad580315b9c77ff52bf447b22fa940 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sjsj001 <>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 08:32:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Auto-tune HTTP/2 stream receive windows

A naive client announces one stream window for every stream, sized for
the fastest one: 64MB by default. A fast sender behind a slow consumer
fills it, so every slow reader holds that much memory; measured on a
two-hop chain with eight downloads read at 1MB/s, each process held
about 645MB. A smaller fixed window costs the fast case instead: 16MB
halved one stream over 180ms (485 to 262Mbit/s).

With auto-tuning on, a session whose transport reports its RTT announces
an initial stream window sized for 1200Mbit/s over the path's RTT, 8MB at
the least. The path's RTT is the smaller of the session's own smoothed
RTT and the minimum RTT its pool's other sessions to the server report:
a session opened while the path already queues measures that queue in
its handshake (1.8s behind a saturated 50Mbit/s link in a test), and a
window sized on it would add to the queue. A stream doubles its window
towards the configured size when half of it is consumed within two round
trips of the previous such update (QUIC's rule) twice in a row, and the
smoothed RTT is within 1.5x of the minimum. A slow reader never meets
the first condition, so its window and the memory behind it stay at the
initial size; twice in a row keeps a relay's stream from reading a burst
into the next hop's window as a fast reader; a queue on the path fails
the last, so the window does not grow with the queue it would feed. The
8MB floor covers a local hop, whose RTT is microseconds while its
consumer takes milliseconds.

The RTT comes from TCP_INFO (Linux and Android; tcpi_min_rtt needs
Linux 4.6), through a new StreamSocket::GetTransportRttInfo that the SSL
socket, TCPClientSocket and the custom dialer's socket forward. A
session over a transport that cannot report it announces the configured
window as before. The switch is per engine: the HTTP2Options
experimental option "stream_window_auto_tuning" sets
HttpNetworkSessionParams::spdy_stream_recv_window_auto_tuning, which the
session's SpdySessionPool hands to each session it initializes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
